g on the transistor radio if it  failed the company was doomed but its  success paved the way for other Japanese  electronics companies encouraged by the  sales of the tr-63 Sony's competitors  Toshiba and sharp decided to scale up  their own production of transistor  radios by 1959 there were more than 6  million japanese-made transistor radio  sets in the United States Sony the first  mover Grew From seven employees to 500.  this incredible success showed other  Japanese firms and to miti that they  should invest in transistor production  by 1960 Japanese transistor production  reached parity with the United States it  was just one of the first triumphs of an  epic decade in 1960 prime minister Akita  announced a plan to use tax breaks  infrastructure investment and Export  incentives to double the size of the  country's economy in 10 years he did it  in less than seven the plan didn't just  Succeed In the words of one historian it  enshrined economic growthism as a  national secular religion the Japanese  model became a blueprint for Rapid  economic growth it was being studied  closely in South Korea Taiwan and China  and would provide inspiration for their  own economic Miracles by the late 1960s  Japan's recovery was virtually complete  it had risen From the Ashes of World War  II to the first rank of economic powers  for its government and major companies  it was time to shift from recovery to  dominance Japan's economy wasn't just  booming it was maturing simple products  like textiles and lightning  manufacturing decreased in importance  compared to more complex manufactured  goods such as cars ships and electronics  improved industrial capacity was also  filtering down to Consumers suddenly  every Japanese family needed its three  Treasures a refrigerator a washing  machine and a TV the all-powerful miti  which had spent years protecting  Japanese firms by forcing foreign  companies to license their technology to  Japanese producers at discounted rates  as 