se semiconductor  companies which enabled them to sell at  artificially low prices in foreign  markets a trade practice known as  dumping by 1985 America's trade deficit  with Japan stood at about 50 billion  dollars but the community and political  leaders didn't always express their  anger in the right ways one notorious  incident occurred when a Toshiba  subsidiary secretly sold the Soviet  Union equipment that made its submarines  quieter and therefore more dangerous to  the U.S Navy in response nine members of  Congress assembled at the U.S Capitol  and smashed a Toshiba Boombox with  sledgehammers the footage was played  over and over in Japan as an example of  American intolerance Japan bashing  resonated with a wide swath of the  American public in the 1980s from car  companies in Detroit to electronics  manufacturers in Silicon Valley American  manufacturing was declining and Japan  was the culprit at least according to  politicians eager to find someone to  blame here's a typical quote from back  then we have to stop showing that white  flag to start running up the American  flag and to turn around fight and make  America number one again in  international Commerce so that American  jobs are filled in this country if we  don't get cracking get serious and get  leadership our kids will be cheated by  us who do you think said this Donald  Trump no it wasn't him although he was  saying this kind of stuff it was Walter  Mondale the Democratic Presidential  nominee in 1984. it wasn't all anger  there was also some admiration of what  Japan had accomplished popular books  with titles like miti and the Japanese  Miracle and Trading Places argued that  America needed to adapt its own  industrial policy to keep up with Japan  in the latter the author Clyde  prestowitz even wrote that the Japanese  Juggernaut cannot stop of its own  volition for Japan has created a kind of  automatic wealth machine perhaps the  first since King Midas a famous American  sociologist E