ur  times the value of all property in the  United States at the time on December 29  1989 Japan's stock market index peaked  at 39  916 points almost five times higher than  at the start of the decade it has never  come close to that Mark since by August  1990 it had plummeted to half its value  other assets also began to to collapse  in desperation the bank of Japan lifted  interest rates in an attempt to take air  out of the bubble but it was too late  starved of capital and suddenly forced  to repay loans with high interest rates  many Japanese companies collapsed but an  asset price crash is only half of the  story of Japan's collapse the other half  of the story is about an inability to  respond to change by the late 1980s and  early 1990s other nations especially  Japan's East Asian neighbors were  gaining ground taiwan's semiconductor  industry led by tsmc began to gain  market share in South Korea new  conglomerates such as Samsung Hyundai LG  and others were contributing to that  country's own economic Miracle helped  Along by lower labor costs and better  targeted government support for business  r d and investment South Korea caught up  and largely surpassed Japan in  semiconductors all while China's economy  was being led from the doldrums by Deng  xiaopeng they had studied Japan's  industrial policies closely and they  were applying the lessons other  countries were catching up and eating  into Japan's market share just as it had  done in Prior decades Japan's government  and Central Bank failed at Key moments  so did its companies they stopped  innovating and they expanded into areas  that were either a distraction like  insurance and movie studios or not part  of their Core Business Like nuclear  power plants they were arrogant and  dismissive about competition from Korean  and Chinese electronics companies the  biggest reason for the failure of  Japanese companies however is the  business model that allowed the kariatsu  to grow so large in the first place  close collaboration with the government  a heavy focus on traditional Industries  and a reluctance to admit foreign  companies people and ideas these  strategies couldn't confront two major  developments in the global economy the  decentralization of Supply chains and  software let's start with the first  throughout much of the 20th century  Supply chains were centralized that  rewarded vertically integrated  manufacturers who could control the  production process from research and  find financing through to manufacturing  final assembly and distribution this  helps explain the success of Industries  like car making in Detroit shipbuilding  in England and electronics Manufacturing  in Japan globalization which is the  liberalization of trade barriers and  improvements in transportation and  Communications changed all of that today  production is highly modular and Supply  chains are Global that prioritizes  flexibility and means Capital flows to  lower cost jurisdictions manufacturers  make greater use of Outsourcing and  offshore their operations as well as buy  from foreign suppliers decentralized  manufacturing undermine the competitive  advantage of Japan's keretsu which  relied on long-term relationships with  suppliers Banks and workers it's  interesting to compare Japan's continued  strength in products characterized by  internal production like cars and  cameras to its weaknesses in those that  have shifted to modular production such  as smartphones there's really no better  example than semiconductors as we  learned in my previous video about tsmc  its founder Morris Chang Bay basically  made one big bet he was going to build a  pure play Foundry a company that did  nothing but make chips tsmc helped break  apart what had been a highly centralized  industry good news for them and the  hundreds of startups that could  Outsource their manufacturing but bad  news for Japan the second part of the  transformation in manufacturing was the  rise of software you might think that  Japan would excel in software after all  they brought the world Kaizen the  practice of continuous incremental  Improvement but Japan hasn't been able  to operate on the Leading Edge of  software development many economists  actually pointed Japan's weakness in  software as the single biggest factor in  the country's competitive decline  relative to the United States one study  found that U.S firms that improved their  performance over the 1990s made the  biggest improvements in the areas where  software was most critical think about  so many of the electronics Goods that we  associate with the era of Japanese  dominance personal entertainment devices  like the Sony Walkman or the Nintendo  Game Boy cameras made by companies like  Nikon reliable efficient cars like the  Toyota Corolla these are all great great  products that Drew on Japan's  superiority and craft manufacturing but  they were all fundamentally mechanical  products they relied on Hardware not  software so as software changed our  tastes and created a new class of  products many of these mechanical goods  and the companies that made them were  left behind think about it do you own a  stand-alone radio or digital camera  today or do you just use your American  or chinese-made smartphone what about  your TV was it made in Japan or South  Korea Japan's economic collapse in the  1990s was so devastating and traumatic  that the Japanese people refer to it as  the Lost decade although today it's  probably more accurate to say lost  decades what did it look like let's  paint a picture Japan's economic growth  slowed almost to half its market share  in critical Industries like  semiconductors plummeted and it meant  the Japanese electronics companies which  had been ascended until recently started  to become Irrelevant in 2005 when Apple  built the iPod Steve Jobs needed to be  in Japan regularly because Japanese  companies produce 7 70 percent of the  parts there were some of Apple's 