se semiconductor  companies which enabled them to sell at  artificially low prices in foreign  markets a trade practice known as  dumping by 1985 America's trade deficit  with Japan stood at about 50 billion  dollars but the community and political  leaders didn't always express their  anger in the right ways one notorious  incident occurred when a Toshiba  subsidiary secretly sold the Soviet  Union equipment that made its submarines  quieter and therefore more dangerous to  the U.S Navy in response nine members of  Congress assembled at the U.S Capitol  and smashed a Toshiba Boombox with  sledgehammers the footage was played  over and over in Japan as an example of  American intolerance Japan bashing  resonated with a wide swath of the  American public in the 1980s from car  companies in Detroit to electronics  manufacturers in Silicon Valley American  manufacturing was declining and Japan  was the culprit at least according to  politicians eager to find someone to  blame here's a typical quote from back  then we have to stop showing that white  flag to start running up the American  flag and to turn around fight and make  America number one again in  international Commerce so that American  jobs are filled in this country if we  don't get cracking get serious and get  leadership our kids will be cheated by  us who do you think said this Donald  Trump no it wasn't him although he was  saying this kind of stuff it was Walter  Mondale the Democratic Presidential  nominee in 1984. it wasn't all anger  there was also some admiration of what  Japan had accomplished popular books  with titles like miti and the Japanese  Miracle and Trading Places argued that  America needed to adapt its own  industrial policy to keep up with Japan  in the latter the author Clyde  prestowitz even wrote that the Japanese  Juggernaut cannot stop of its own  volition for Japan has created a kind of  automatic wealth machine perhaps the  first since King Midas a famous American  sociologist Ezra Vogel published a book  called Japan as number one lessons for  America however even as these books were  being published and the boom boxes were  being smashed storm clouds were  gathering on the horizon let's quickly  recap just 40 years on from the utter  Devastation of World War II Japan had  grown into the second largest economy in  the world by pursuing a state-led  industrialization policy that emphasized  close links between manufacturers  suppliers and the banking system and  protection in the form of tariffs and  Technology transfer requirements  Japanese firms grew strong at home and  made inroads in international markets in  1989 32 of the world's top 50 companies  by market capitalization were Japanese  but when you spend years pumping up your  economy you get bubbles and what happens  to Bubbles they burst much has been  written about the causes of Japan's  economic decline the real answer is that  structural long-term weaknesses were  compounded by acute short-term factors  let's begin with the bubble Japan  suffered the indako recession in the  mid-1980s which most economists today  agree was caused by a rapid appreciation  in the value of its currency the Yen the  appreciation of the Yen undercut Japan's  traditional Advantage exports and  capital investment this had been the  cause of so much of its economic  strength between the 1960s and the 1980s  the Japanese government responded by  adopting an expansive fiscal policy at  the same time the bank of Japan which  had declared curbing the Yen's  appreciation and National priority  pursued aggressive monetary easing  eventually after repeated interest rate  cuts the Yen began to drop in value  mission accomplished except not quite  because the bank of Japan's policies  hadn't just engineered a currency  depreciation they had also hugely  inflated asset values at the height of  the market all of the land in Japan a  country the size of California was worth  about 18 trillion dollars or almost four  times the value of all property in the  United States at the time on December 29  1989 Japan's stock market index peaked  at 39  916 points almost five times higher than  at the start of the decade it has never  come close to that Mark since by August  1990 it had plummeted to half its value  other assets also began to to collapse  in desperation the bank of Japan lifted  interest rates in an attempt to take air  out of the bubble but it was too late  starved of capital and suddenly forced  to repay loans with high interest rates  many Japanese companies collapsed but an  asset price crash is only half of the  story of Japan's collapse the other half  of the story is about an inability to  respond to change by the late 1980s and  early 1990s other nations especially  Japan's East Asian neighbors were  gaining ground taiwan's semiconductor  industry led by tsmc began to gain  market share in South Korea new  conglomerates such as Samsung Hyundai LG  and others were contributing to that  country's own economic Miracle helped  Along by lower labor costs and better  targeted government support for business  r d and investment South Korea caught up  and largely surpassed Japan in  semiconductors all while China's economy  was being led from the doldrums by Deng  xiaopeng they had studied Japan's  industrial policies closely and they  were applying the lessons other  countries were catching up and eating  into Japan's market share just as it had  done in Prior decades Japan's government  and Central Bank failed at Key moments  so did its companies they stopped  innovating and they expanded into areas  that were either a distraction like  insurance and movie studios or not part  of their Core Business Like nuclear  power plants they were arrogant and  dismissive about competition from Korean  and Chinese electronics companies the  biggest reason for the failure of  Japanese companies however is the  business model that allowed the kariatsu  to grow so large in the first place  close collaboration with the government  a heavy focus on traditional Industries  and a reluctance to admit foreign  companies people and ideas these  strategies couldn't confront two major  developments in the global economy the  decentralization of Supply chains and  software let's start with the first  throughout much of the 20th century  Supply chains were centralized that  rewarded vertically integrated  manufacturers who could control the  production process from research and  find financing through to manufacturing  final assembly and distribution this  helps explain the success of Industries  like car making in Detroit shipbuilding  in England and electronics Manufacturing  in Japan globalization which is the  liberalization of trade barriers and  improvements in transportation and  Communications changed all of that today  production is highly modular and Supply  chains are Global that prioritizes  flexibility and means Capital flows to  lower cost jurisdictions manufacturers  make greater use of Outsourcing and  offshore their operations as well as buy  from foreign suppliers decentralized  manufacturing undermine the competitive  advantage of Japan's keretsu which  relied on long-term relationships with  suppliers Banks and workers it's  interesting to compare Japan's continued  strength in products characterized by  internal production like cars and  cameras to its weaknesses in those that  have shifted to modular production such  as smartphones there's really no better  example than semiconductors as we  learned in my previous video about tsmc  its founder Morris Chang Bay basically  made one big bet he was going to build a  pure play Foundry a company that did  nothing but make chips tsmc helped break  apart what had been a highly centralized  industry good news for them and the  hundreds of startups that could  Outsource their manufacturing but bad  news for Japan the second part of the  transformation in manufacturing was the  rise of software you might think that  Japan would excel in software after all  they brought the world Kaizen the  practice of continuous incremental  Improvement but Japan hasn't been able  to operate on the Leading Edge of  software development many economists  actually pointed Japan's weakness in  software as the single biggest factor in  the country's competitive decline  relative to the United States one study  found that U.S firms that improved their  performance over the 1990s made the  biggest improvements in the areas where  software was most critical think about  so many of the electronics Goods that we  associate with the era of Japanese  dominance personal entertainment devices  like the Sony Walkman or the Nintendo  Game Boy cameras made by companies like  Nikon reliable efficient cars like the  Toyota Corolla these are all great great  products that Drew on Japan's  superiority and craft manufacturing but  they were all fundamentally mechanical  products they relied on Hardware not  software so as software changed our  tastes and created a new class of  products many of these mechanical goods  and the companies that made them were  left behind think about it do you own a  stand-alone radio or digital camera  today or do you just use your American  or chinese-made smartphone what about  your TV was it made in Japan or South  Korea Japan's economic collapse in the  1990s was so devastating and traumatic  that the Japanese people refer to it as  the Lost decade although today it's  probably more accurate to say lost  decades what did it look like let's  paint a picture Japan's economic growth  slowed almost to half its market share  in critical Industries like  semiconductors plummeted and it meant  the Japanese electronics companies which  had been ascended until recently started  to become Irrelevant in 2005 when Apple  built the iPod Steve Jobs needed to be  in Japan regularly because Japanese  companies produce 7 70 percent of the  parts there were some of Apple's key  suppliers but just five years later  Apple had shifted their manufacturing  strategy away from Japan and toured  China this led to the iPad using only 20  Japanese Parts remember how I said  earlier that in 1989 32 of the world's  top 50 companies by market cap were  Japanese well by 2018 there was just one  Toyota the central preoccupation of  Japanese politics since the last decade  has been to revive the dynamic culture  of economic growth that was once a  secular religion but despite the best  efforts of leaders like the late Abe  Shinzo who spent his second prime  ministership trying to stimulate the  economy the sleeping giant wouldn't wake  why because in addition to those huge  changes I mentioned there are other  factors which weigh heavily against  Japan the first is demographics Japan is  really old it sells more adult diapers  than baby diapers the baby boom  generation born in the late 1940s and  50s is retiring and there are enough  young people to replace them in 2014  Japan's population was 127 million at  current rates that's expected to shift  to 107 Million by 2040 and 97 Million by  2050. how can you be expected to be at  the Forefront of the ideas and  Technology shaping the future if you're  too old to take advantage of those  opportunities Japan's demographic  challenge is closely related to another  problem the country's reluctance to  admit new people and new ideas those  people bring the US isn't perfect but it  still boasts an unrivaled culture of  dynamism and optimism built largely on  the contributions of skilled migrants  who want to improve their lives and  contribute to the next generation of  Innovations the other is that having now  been in Decline for as long as it was on  the rise Japan has a lot of ground to  make up in order to catch countries like  the United States and China Guided by a  Visionary and vigorous government  working in tandem with the industrial  and banking sectors there was a time  when it looked like Japan and Japanese  technology companies might rule the  world that era of dominance is over  Japan today faces massive challenges  let's hope that like it once did it can  overcome them and create another  economic miracle thanks for watching
 

